# uConsole Raspberry Pi kernel

This repository contains the Linux kernel source used by the
[uConsole Ubuntu LTS](https://github.com/blue-1ms/uconsole-ubuntu-lts) project for the
ClockworkPi uConsole CM4 Lite.

The current hardware-passed release is:

- stable tag: `7.1.3-stable`
- source commit: `4bad9ea6781a9dde63322956bba4c10070957aaf`
- runtime ABI: `7.1.3-1003-uconsole`
- package release: [uConsole kernel 7.1.3 stable](https://github.com/blue-1ms/uconsole-apt/releases/tag/7.1.3-stable)

Do not build releases from a floating branch. Every package release is bound to an exact source
commit, patch inventory, final configuration, ABI, toolchain, and immutable receipt in the
control-plane repository.

## Branch model

- `main` is the repository landing and maintenance branch. It is not a release identifier.
- `uconsole/ubuntu-26.04` is the exact source line used for the current Ubuntu 26.04 kernel.
- Future maintained kernel lines use a version-specific `uconsole/<suite>-<upstream>` branch.
- `vendor/akrex-7.1.3` is an immutable provenance snapshot used to audit ClockworkPi hardware
  support. It is not an active release base.
- `vendor/akrex-6.12`, `uconsole/6.12-recovery`, and legacy `rpi-*` branches are historical
  references. The 6.12 recovery kernel is no longer built or delivered.

Release tags are immutable:

- candidate: `<upstream>-candidate.<NN>`
- hardware-passed promotion: `<upstream>-stable`

Promotion reuses the exact candidate package bytes. Tags, packages, and receipts are never
overwritten.

## AK-Rex provenance

AK-Rex source remains credited and preserved because the uConsole panel, backlight, power/PMIC,
battery, and device-tree work was originally audited from that source. The project does not
track an AK-Rex branch as its current kernel base and does not copy AK-Rex binary packages into
new releases.

For a new upstream kernel:

1. start from an exact Canonical Ubuntu `linux-raspi` source commit;
2. rebase and review the uConsole patch series maintained by `uconsole-ubuntu-lts`;
3. compare against the pinned AK-Rex snapshot only for provenance or newly discovered hardware
   fixes;
4. build and validate signed Debian packages through the control plane;
5. install through `flash-kernel` and `piboot-try`, retaining exactly one N-1 fallback kernel.

## Build and release

The supported build interface, manifests, package validation, A/B boot policy, hardware
checklist, and release evidence are maintained in:

- [uConsole Ubuntu LTS control plane](https://github.com/blue-1ms/uconsole-ubuntu-lts)
- [signed uConsole APT repository](https://github.com/blue-1ms/uconsole-apt)

Kernel source remains licensed under GPL-2.0-only. See `COPYING` for the kernel license and the
control-plane `NOTICE` for third-party provenance.
